  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM EDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nThe area is bookended by two areas of vorticity to start this \nMonday, but the stalled pattern shears out the vorticity to our west \nand the nor'easter over the Northeast pushes out to sea during the \nday today. This leaves the area under surface high pressure and mid \nlevel ridging suppressing activity and keeping the area dry today. \nThis continues a 3 day streak of seeing MinRH values reach 30 \npercent overhead indicating how dry it has gotten each day. We \nsecurely reached into the 70s for highs on Sunday and much of our \nsouthwest zones and portions of NW OH saw 75F highs. This \ncombination of dry air and seasonable highs is expected to continue \nfor today with another day of low to mid 70s for highs (some areas \nmay stretch into the upper 70s) and dew points in the 50s that \nmay attempt to mix back into the 40s during peak heating. The \none thing that could stop mixing today is some high clouds \noverhead.\n\nAt the same time, a trough moves into the western US and pushes \neastward. The northern stream trough shears out from the southern \nstream and a cutoff low forms across the southwest. This causes the \nstronger vorticity to pass by north of the area where the better jet \ndynamics reside. This allows another dry day for Tuesday and helps \ntrend Wednesday away from being wet to more of a dry day. NAM Bufkit \nsoundings at BEH indicate a very dry layer below 850 mb through all \nof Wednesday.\n\nFor Thursday into Friday, the upper low in Central Canada pushes a \ntrough southeastward and this helps push the sheared out vorticity \ninto the area. This also pushes better moisture content into the \narea and so confidence is higher on finally getting a return to \nrain. Even still, surface moisture doesn't appear as hydrated as it \ncould be, instability is very low, and the trough is rather \nprogressive through the day Thursday indicating rain that doesn't \nappear all that heavy. \n\nNow that that trough is through the area by day's end Friday, the \ncutoff low down in the southwest doesn't have the necessary kick to \nget it up to the area and so it continues to remain down in the \nsouthwest. There is uncertainty between the GFS and ECMWF on whether \nto bring the energy/moisture from this low northward this weekend. \nEven the AIGFS is between the two in a camp closer to the CMC, which \nbrings it into the area early, but keeps it south of the storm track \nof the ECMWF.",
